---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL;
---
<BaseLayout title="Interactive Code Demos | Technical Documentation Portfolio" description="Interactive examples of API integration, form validation, semantic HTML, responsive CSS, and jQuery.">
  <section class="page-hero wrap"><p class="eyebrow">03 / INTERACTIVE CODE DEMONSTRATIONS</p><h1>Code examples that show both behavior and implementation choices.</h1><p class="lede">These small demonstrations focus on useful front-end fundamentals: accessible forms, asynchronous requests, clear feedback, responsive layouts, and maintainable markup. The weather demo uses public APIs and does not require an API key.</p></section>
  <section class="section wrap" id="weather"><div class="section-heading"><p class="eyebrow">DEMO 01 · API INTEGRATION</p><h2>OpenWeather API: ZIP-code weather lookup</h2><p>This is my original working demo. It uses jQuery to validate a five-digit US ZIP code, geocode the location, request current conditions and forecast data, and display weather alerts when available. The existing page also documents the implementation and its trade-offs.</p><div class="actions"><a class="button primary" href="https://mikekelley89.github.io/openWeatherApi/demos/openweather.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Open the live weather demo ↗</a><a class="button secondary" href="https://github.com/mikekelley89/openWeatherApi" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">View source repository ↗</a></div></div>
    <div class="weather-embed-shell"><iframe class="weather-demo-frame" src="https://mikekelley89.github.io/openWeatherApi/demos/openweather.html" title="Mike Kelley's OpenWeather API demonstration" loading="lazy" referrerpolicy="strict-origin-when-cross-origin"></iframe></div>
    <p class="small-note">The embedded page is hosted separately in my <a href="https://github.com/mikekelley89/openWeatherApi" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">openWeatherApi repository</a>. If the live demo does not display inside this frame, use the button above to open it directly.</p>
    <details class="implementation-notes"><summary>What this example demonstrates</summary><ul><li>Client-side validation for a five-digit US ZIP code.</li><li>Chained asynchronous API requests: geocoding first, then weather data using latitude and longitude.</li><li>Dynamic rendering of conditions, temperature, wind, forecast summary, and available weather alerts.</li><li>Documented implementation choices, limitations, and lessons learned alongside the working example.</li></ul></details>
    <p class="small-note"><strong>Security note:</strong> Before sharing the source broadly, review the OpenWeather API key used by the original public demo. Browser-side keys are visible to visitors; use provider restrictions and usage limits, and rotate the key if it is no longer safe to expose.</p>
  </section>
  <section class="section band"><div class="wrap"><div class="section-heading"><p class="eyebrow">DEMO 02 · HTML / CSS</p><h2>Responsive content layout</h2><p>A standalone sample illustrating semantic structure, responsive behavior, and content-first layout.</p><a class="button secondary" href={`${base}samples/responsive-layout.html`}>Open responsive layout sample ↗</a></div>
  <div class="sample-grid"><article class="sample"><span class="tag">Structure</span><h3>Semantic HTML</h3><p>Meaningful headings, landmarks, lists, and links make content easier to navigate and maintain.</p></article><article class="sample"><span class="tag">Presentation</span><h3>Responsive CSS</h3><p>Flexible grids and breakpoints adapt the layout to different viewport widths.</p></article><article class="sample"><span class="tag">Accessibility</span><h3>Usable by default</h3><p>Readable contrast, visible focus, and logical document structure support more readers.</p></article></div></div></section>
  <section class="section wrap"><div class="section-heading"><p class="eyebrow">DEMO 03 · JAVASCRIPT / JQUERY</p><h2>Progressive enhancement</h2><p>This sample demonstrates interaction and DOM updates with jQuery. It is a small standalone example, separate from the Astro site.</p><a class="button secondary" href={`${base}samples/jquery-example.html`}>Open jQuery sample ↗</a></div><div class="code-panel"><pre><code>$('#search-form').on('submit', function (event) &#123;
  event.preventDefault();
  const query = $('#search').val().trim();

  if (!query) &#123;
    $('#message').text('Enter a search term.');
    return;
  &#125;

  $('#message').text('Ready to search for: ' + query);
&#125;);</code></pre><p class="small-note">Illustrative code snippet. The linked standalone sample contains the runnable implementation.</p></div></section>
  <section class="section band"><div class="wrap"><h2>Engineering decisions worth calling out</h2><div class="check-grid"><p><strong>Input validation</strong><br />Reject malformed input before making a network request.</p><p><strong>Loading state</strong><br />Tell the user what is happening and prevent duplicate submissions.</p><p><strong>Error handling</strong><br />Provide useful feedback when a service is unavailable or returns no result.</p><p><strong>Accessible updates</strong><br />Use labels and live regions so dynamic feedback is perceivable.</p></div></div></section>

</BaseLayout>
